though. My recent work involved removing those explicit Spring Boot starter 
dependencies, and this failure is direct confirmation that those starters are 
indeed required for `task-web` to compile correctly.

Crucially, this *also* validates my hypothesis about the *previous* error. The 
earlier issue where `spring-boot-starter-webmvc` was reported as missing wasn't 
due to any direct mistake I made with my own dependencies. Instead, it points to
an environmental hiccup that was masking the true dependency requirement. The 
current compilation errors, which are precisely what I'd expect without the 
starters, confirm that the environment is now allowing the dependency resolution
to happen correctly, revealing the *actual* missing pieces.

My next step is clear: I need to reintroduce the essential Spring Boot starters 
into the `task-web/pom.xml` file to resolve these compilation issues. I'll be 
adding back:

*   `spring-boot-starter-webmvc` – this is the core for web functionality.
*   `spring-boot-starter-validation` – needed for the validation annotations to 
be recognized.
*   `spring-boot-starter-actuator` – good practice for monitoring and management
endpoints.
*   `spring-boot-starter-test` – naturally, for any testing capabilities, and 
I'll scope this appropriately to `test`.

When I add these, I won't be specifying versions for them. This is intentional 
because the parent POM's `dependencyManagement` section now correctly points to 
the `spring-boot-dependencies` Bill of Materials (BOM). This means the versions 
will be inherited properly, ensuring consistency.

