Summary: Add spring-boot-starter to task-service/pom.xml

Reasoning:
Alright, so I'm seeing a positive development here: that pesky "missing version"
error for `spring-boot-starter-webmvc` in `task-web` has vanished. This confirms
my suspicion that explicitly declaring the versions for those starters was 
indeed the fix for that particular, somewhat opaque problem. Good to have that 
one behind me.

However, that success is immediately followed by a new set of issues, this time 
in `task-service`. The build is now failing with compilation errors, 
specifically complaining that `package org.springframework.stereotype does not 
exist` and `cannot find symbol symbol: class Service`. This is a classic 
indication that the `task-service` module is lacking a fundamental Spring 
dependency that provides the `@Service` annotation and, by extension, the 
`org.springframework.stereotype` package. In my experience, this usually comes 
bundled with `spring-context` or, more broadly, a `spring-boot-starter`.

When I examine the `task-service/pom.xml`, I see it's only declaring 
`task-domain` and `spring-boot-starter-test` as its direct dependencies. It 
clearly needs a more foundational Spring dependency to get its basic compilation
done, particularly for the service layer annotations.

Therefore, the logical next step is to add `spring-boot-starter` to the 
`task-service/pom.xml`. My expectation is that this will pull in all the 
essential Spring Core dependencies, including `spring-context` and the 
`spring-stereotype` package, resolving these compilation errors. I'll add it 
without a version specified, letting the parent's Bill of Materials (BOM) manage
the versioning, which is the standard and cleaner approach.

